{"id": "e7dff341-878b-4a73-95bb-910e78d28268", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T04:19:00.491710", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "cdb0bcc4-dc2e-4d76-9408-672c014ba1b5", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-abc2e6a4-92e1-442e-b9d4-ef894abfa708", "tool_call": {"id": "call-abc2e6a4-92e1-442e-b9d4-ef894abfa708", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Scope only: repo inspection, concretize question, approach sketch\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"\\n# 1) Repository inspection\\n\\n## What `ls -la /workspace` actually shows\\nThe workspace is effectively a blank sheet. Only one file exists:\\n```\\n$ ls -la /workspace\\ndrwx------  2 ... 4096 Oct 23 11:17 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 12 ...  240 Oct 23 11:17 ..\\n-rw-------  1 ...  482 Oct 23 11:17 task.json\\n```\\n\\n## Tree (depth 2)\\nThere is no depth 2 to traverse. `find /workspace -maxdepth 3` returns only `/workspace/task.json` and no subdirectories. [FACT: confirmed via shell]\\n\\n## `track.md`\\n**No `track.md` file exists on disk** — `find / -name \\\"track.md\\\"` returns nothing under `/`. However, `task.json` declares a `documents.track.md` field, whose value is the literal one-line string:\\n> \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\nThat string is the closest thing to track.md that exists. Reading it as a meta-instruction: this task is **design-only**; no winning state requires an executed experiment or installed dependencies. I'm taking that as binding.\\n\\n## Inherited code / scaffolding / configs\\nNone. Specifically absent on disk [FACT]:\\n- No game code, no game assets, no map/state files.\\n- No agent scaffolding (no policy, no `agent.py`, no `env.py`, no `train.py`).\\n- No RL framework — no Stable-Baselines / Ray RLlib / CleanRL / Tianshou / Acme / Dopamine.\\n- No simulator wrapper — no Gym/Gymnasium env, no PyBoy/Retro, no Unity/Unreal bindings, no game server client.\\n- No config files — no `config.yaml`, no `hydra` tree, no CLI tools.\\n- No datasets, no pretrained checkpoints, no logs.\\n\\nYou are designing **on a blank sheet**. Any \\\"agent\\\" / \\\"game\\\" code referenced by the design has to be authored from scratch as part of the design deliverable (or specified as a normative spec).\\n\\n## Environment reality (empirically checked, not assumed)\\n\\n| Item | Result | Source |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Python | 3.12.3 | `python3 --version` |\\n| stdlib (`json`, `random`, `math`, `statistics`, `itertools`, `multiprocessing`, `pickle`) | works | `python3 -c \\\"import ...\\\"` |\\n| numpy / scipy | **not installed** | `ModuleNotFoundError` |\\n| torch (incl. CUDA) | **not installed** | `ModuleNotFoundError` |\\n| gymnasium / pettingzoo | **not installed** | `ModuleNotFoundError` |\\n| pip | present (24.0) but useless | DNS to pypi.org fails |\\n| Network | **disabled** | DNS resolution fails (`socket.gaierror`) |\\n| GPU | n/a (no torch + no CUDA env) | import probe |\\n| CPUs schedulable | 24 | `os.sched_getaffinity(0)` and `nproc` agree |\\n| Memory | 125 Gi total / 102 Gi available | `free -h` |\\n\\n**Implication for any design:** pure stdlib Python only is what survives. Any plan that needs pip-install, GPU compute, or live game telemetry is not deliverable in this sandbox. I'm building everything off stdlib.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 2) Concretizing the question\\n\\n## Read of the brief, one paragraph\\nThe brief asks whether a class of **environment-side events** (\\\"pirate invasions\\\" triggered by a **score-threshold rule**) changes an outcome (**survival duration**) differently when the agents playing the game are organized **hierarchically vs. non-hierarchically**. Operationally it's a 2×2 question: does the survival curve under \\\"mechanic-on\\\" differ between two agent architectures, relative to the survival curve under \\\"mechanic-off\\\"? Concretely, *hierarchical* = a stacked controller picking temporally-extended options; *non-hierarchical* = a single flat policy over atomic actions; *mechanic* = a deterministic world-event firing on a state condition, not an agent-chosen action.\\n\\n## What needs pinning down before any design is sensible\\n\\n- **Game / simulator identity.** No game is present in the repo, and \\\"pirate invasions fire on score threshold\\\" is not a recognizable mechanic of any single open-source game I can confidently name without checking the source. Candidates and feasibility in this sandbox:\\n  - **(A) Author a minimal stdlib discrete-event simulator with the mechanic defined as a written rule.** Most defensible here — blank sheet, no network, no native code, no GPU. Loses some external validity but gains total control of the IV. **(Recommended.)**\\n  - (B) FTL-style space roguelike — actually proprietary; no network to install; mechanics don't match (rebel fleet is a timer, not a score threshold; pirates are encounters, not score-triggered invasions). Bad fit on all axes.\\n  - (C) FreeCiv / Stellaris-like 4X — has barbarian/pirate spawns but not score-triggered; binary too heavy to install/run offline.\\n  - (D) Endless Sky / Pioneer Space Sim — open, has pirates, but again \\\"score threshold triggers invasion\\\" isn't a built-in mechanic — would be a custom mod.\\n  - (E) Tiny gridworld + stunt-pirate spawn — equivalent to (A) with different flavor.\\n  I lean (A). If you want a real existing game rather than a synthetic one, name it and I'll redesign around it, but I won't pretend to download one.\\n\\n- **Boundary between \\\"built-in mechanic\\\" and \\\"agent-controlled behavior\\\".** My operational split, which I'd ask you to confirm:\\n  - *Built-in (env-side):* transitions triggered by the simulator state machine without that trigger being the agent's last action. Includes: score-threshold crossings; spawn events from those crossings; pirate-AI moves/attacks in the same env-step; resource ticks; world hazards.\\n  - *Agent-controlled:* transitions where the agent's policy selected the immediate action — move, shoot, evade, repair, drop resource.\\n  - The split lives in `env.step(action)`: anything inside that function not directly caused by `action` is built-in. **The OFF control must keep the score-tracking logic intact and only suppress the *spawn side-effect*** (otherwise you're confounding \\\"no mechanic\\\" with \\\"different economy\\\"). If you disagree with where the line goes, say so now — every later decision hinges on it.\\n\\n- **\\\"Hierarchical\\\" vs \\\"non-hierarchical\\\" — operational meaning, picking one.** Options:\\n  - **(i) Options / Feudal-style temporal hierarchy** — meta-policy picks among options (temporally-extended sub-policies), each ending in a termination condition. Non-hierarchical = flat policy over atomic actions. **Operationalization:** identical observation space, identical atomic action space; only the decision procedure differs.\\n  - (ii) Module decomposition (separate networks for navigation/combat/resources orchestrated by a hand-coded dispatcher) — conflates software structure with policy structure.\\n  - (iii) Multi-agent with explicit commander / sub-commander — conflates with multi-agentism.\\n  - (iv) Pure temporal abstraction without meta-controller — subset of (i).\\n  I pick **(i)** because it's the only one where the two conditions differ in *decision structure alone*, with observation/action spaces held constant. That is what isolates the IV. (ii)/(iii) mix in extra degrees of freedom; (iv) collapses into (i). Tell me if \\\"hierarchical\\\" in your head means module decomposition, and I'll re-plan — but I'm not blending interpretations.\\n\\n- **Open items I refuse to silently guess**:\\n  - Whether agents **learn** (RL with reward) during episodes, or are evaluated with **fixed** (random / hand-scripted / pre-computed) policies. Affects training-time budget dramatically.\\n  - Whether **score** is identical across ON / OFF (must be yes to isolate the IV) or scales with condition. I'm assuming identical; please confirm.\\n  - **Survival duration units** — ticks / steps / wall-clock / scenario-time. I'm proposing ticks.\\n  - **Death conditions** — pirate-induced damage only, or do natural causes (starvation, random hazards) also end episodes? The ON/OFF effect size depends on this.\\n  - Whether the threshold is fixed or stochastic. I'd default to fixed deterministic for v1.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 3) Proposed approach sketch (shape only — not the design)\\n\\n## Factor structure (one line)\\n**Factor A — Agent architecture ∈ {Hierarchical, Non-hierarchical}; Factor B — Mechanic triggering ∈ {ON, OFF}** ⇒ four cells, fully crossed.\\n\\n## Primary dependent variable (with units)\\n**Survival duration in simulator ticks** (integer count of env-steps from `reset()` to termination). Secondary DVs (informational, not hypothesis-bearing): cause-of-death class, score at termination, cumulative damage received, number of pirate encounters survived.\\n\\n## Planned control conditions\\n- **OFF cell:** same simulator, identical score-tracking code, **the score-threshold check evaluates but the spawn side-effect is suppressed**. Same RNG stream for score progression. This is the matched-everything-else control that isolates *the trigger's spawn behavior*, not the existence of score.\\n- **Non-hierarchical baseline:** flat policy over the same atomic action set the hierarchical agent's sub-policies ultimately call. Where the hierarchical agent might terminate an option (\\\"flee ended at safe tile\\\"), the non-hierarchical agent must produce the same atomic sequence by direct action selection, but with no termination/meta gating.\\n- **Pair-matched seeding:** each replicate in Hierarchical runs paired with a Non-hierarchical replicate initialized from the **same RNG seed** so observable rollout differences come from architecture, not stochasticity.\\n\\n## Measurement window / stopping rule\\n- Per episode: hard cap (proposing 5,000 ticks; will tune to ~10× expected median survival to avoid ceiling effects).\\n- Across replicates: fixed **n = 30 per cell** (120 episodes total) as the minimum-feasible anchor, with a planned power-analysis footnote in the design. Will revisit n if variance in a 3-episode pilot suggests more is needed.\\n- Aggregate stopping rule: terminate per-cell collection when the 95% CI half-width on mean survival is < 10% of the cross-cell pooled mean, or n hits cap.\\n\\n## Randomization & replication under 24 cores, no network\\n- `multiprocessing.Pool(24)` — one run per worker task; results pickled back.\\n- 120 episodes / 24 workers ≈ 5 waves. Workers are stateless; each takes a `(seed, factor_A, factor_B)` tuple.\\n- Seeds: master seed → Mersenne Twister-derived sub-seeds, recorded in a manifest so the run is reproducible from disk alone.\\n- No I/O during episodes; only final per-episode summaries hit disk to avoid any lock contention.\\n\\n## Consequential assumptions and unknowns to carry into design\\n\\n- **[ASSUMPTION]** No game exists in repo → the design specifies a **minimal, fully-described simulator** with the score-threshold mechanic written down as a rule. Folding this into the design deliverable is on the critical path.\\n- **[ASSUMPTION]** Stdlib-only implementation is sufficient. No torch / numpy / gym. Tabular or linear-function agents are the realistic substrate.\\n- **[ASSUMPTION]** Score-tracking is identical in ON and OFF cells; only the spawn side-effect differs.\\n- **[ASSUMPTION]** Pair-matched seeds across hierarchical / non-hierarchical within replicate isolate architecture as the only decision-side IV.\\n- **[ASSUMPTION]** \\\"Hierarchical\\\" = options-style temporal hierarchy (meta-policy + sub-policies with termination conditions) vs. flat-atomics — pending your confirmation; I won't blend interpretations.\\n- **[ASSUMPTION]** Survival duration is best measured in ticks; aggregated statistics use mean / median + CI, not rank-based alone.\\n- **[ASSUMPTION]** Agents are **fixed policies** for v1 (randomized scripted or hand-designed) unless you say RL is required — RL training changes the runtime budget by orders of magnitude and may push against the budget.\\n- **[ASSUMPTION]** Single agent per episode — no peer agents.\\n- **[UNKNOWN]** Whether RL must be in-scope or fixed-policy evaluation is acceptable.\\n- **[UNKNOWN]** Whether the researcher's mental model of \\\"hierarchical\\\" is options/FuN/Feudal vs. module decomposition vs. multi-agent command structure.\\n- **[UNKNOWN]** The exact death-condition set (pirate damage only, or include natural causes).\\n- **[UNKNOWN]** Whether the threshold should be deterministic or stochastic; whether threshold value should match expected median survival or sit elsewhere.\\n- **[UNKNOWN]** Whether qualitative reports of failure modes (what kind of death, what the score curve looked like) are expected as a *design output* alongside the quantitative result, or strictly out of scope.\\n- **[UNKNOWN]** Acceptable per-cell n — I've proposed 30 as a defensible floor; this is a design input, not a fixed number.\\n\\n---\\n\\nStopping here as instructed. No full design, no analysis code, no fabricated results. Awaiting your read on the labeled assumptions and unknowns before I draft the design proper.\\n\",\"evidence\":\"\\n- `ls -la /workspace` output shows only `task.json` (one file, no subdirectories).\\n- `find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -type f` returns only `/workspace/task.json`; `-type d` returns only `/workspace`.\\n- `find / -name \\\"track.md\\\"` returns no results — track.md is not a physical file; `task.json` references it as a documents entry whose value is the string \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" Task is explicit: design-only.\\n- No agent/sim/RL/config code on disk. Confirmed via the empty find result.\\n- Environment probed empirically: Python 3.12.3; stdlib works; numpy/scipy/torch/gymnasium/pettingzoo all `ModuleNotFoundError`; pip present but DNS to pypi.org fails (`socket.gaierror: Temporary failure in name resolution`) → network confirmed disabled; `os.sched_getaffinity(0)` = 24; `free -h` shows 125 Gi total / 102 Gi avail.\\n- Concretization choices grounded in the empty-repo fact: no game to inherit, so an authored minimal stdlib simulator is the only feasible substrate in this sandbox.\\n- \\\"Hierarchical\\\" pinned to options/Feudal temporal hierarchy (rationale: only interpretation that holds observation/action spaces constant, isolating decision structure as the IV). All alternatives (module decomposition, multi-agent command, temporal-only) flagged as either conflating factors or as subsets.\\n- Factor structure is a clean 2×2 (Agent × Mechanic) because the brief contains exactly two IVs and one DV; nothing in the question requires more cells at the sketch stage.\\n- n=30 per cell, 120 total, pair-matched seeds, multiprocessing.Pool(24) — justified by 24 schedulable cores, stdlib-only runtime, and the no-network/no-GPU constraint that rules out deep-RL training costs.\\n\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"n=30 per cell is a defensible floor for v1, to be revisited upward if pilot variance is large.</item>\",\"$text\":\"OFF control uses a matched-everything-else suppression of the spawn — not removal of the score mechanic itself — to keep game economy identical.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Single agent per episode; no peers, no multi-agent interactions.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Agents are fixed policies for v1 (random / scripted); RL training is not in scope unless the researcher says otherwise (massive runtime delta).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Survival duration is measured in simulator ticks; mean and median both reported with 95% CIs.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"\\\"Hierarchical\\\" = options/Feudal temporal hierarchy (meta-policy + sub-policies with termination), not module decomposition and not multi-agent command structure.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Pair-matched seeds across Hierarchical / Non-hierarchical within a replicate so rollout differences are attributable to architecture, not RNG.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Score-tracking is held identical across ON and OFF cells; only the spawn side-effect of the threshold differs.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Stdlib-only Python is sufficient to express both the simulator and the two agent architectures (tabular / linear only — no torch).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"No game is implied by the brief beyond what's on disk; if a specific real game is intended, it isn't installed and can't be installed (no network), so the design must specify one.</item>\\n\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Whether the comparison should also include a no-pirates-no-threshold condition (true baseline) on top of the score-threshold ON/OFF pair, or if matched OFF is sufficient.</item>\",\"$text\":\"Acceptable per-cell n, and whether the CI-half-width stopping rule is the right secondary criterion or should be replaced with a fixed n.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether trajectory-level qualitative failure analyses are wanted as a design output, or strictly quantitative.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the score threshold should be deterministic or stochastic, and where to set it relative to expected survival.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"What terminates an episode — pirate-induced death only, or do natural failures (resource, hazards) also end it.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether \\\"hierarchical\\\" in the researcher's frame means option-based HRL vs. module decomposition vs. multi-agent command structure.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the researcher means real-RL, fixed-policy evaluation, or some scripted baseline by \\\"agent.\\\"</item>\\n\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-82055e29-ed87-4f39-a4a5-3d4ee21da83d", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 16}
