nimal stdlib discrete-event simulator with the mechanic defined as a written rule.** Most defensible here — blank sheet, no network, no native code, no GPU. Loses some external validity but gains total control of the IV. **(Recommended.)**\n  - (B) FTL-style space roguelike — proprietary; no network to install; mechanics don't match (rebel fleet is a timer, not a score threshold; pirates are encounters, not score-triggered invasions). Bad fit on all axes.\n  - (C) FreeCiv / Stellaris-like 4X — has barbarian/pirate spawns but not score-triggered; binaries too heavy to install/run offline.\n  - (D) Endless Sky / Pioneer Space Sim — open, has pirates, but \"score threshold triggers invasion\" isn't a built-in mechanic — would be a custom mod.\n  - (E) Tiny gridworld + stunt-pirate spawn — equivalent to (A) with different flavor.\n  I lean (A). If you want a real existing game rather than a synthetic one, name it and I'll redesign around it, but I won't pretend to download one.\n\n- **Boundary between \"built-in mechanic\" and \"agent-controlled behavior\".** My operational split, pending your confirmation:\n  - *Built-in (env-side):* transitions triggered by the simulator state machine without that trigger being the agent's last action. Includes: score-threshold crossings; spawn events from those crossings; pirate-AI moves/attacks in the same env-step; resource ticks; world hazards.\n  - *Agent-controlled:* transitions where the agent's policy selected the immediate action — move, shoot, evade, repair, drop resource.\n  - The split lives in `env.step(action)`: anything inside that function not directly caused by `action` is built-in. **The OFF control must keep the score-tracking logic intact and only suppress the *spawn side-effect***, otherwise you confound \"no mechanic\" with \"different economy\". If you disagree with where the line goes, say so now — every later decision hinges on it.\n\n- **\"Hierarchical\" vs \"non-hierarchical\" — operational meaning, picking 