{"id": "aea60e97-ed37-4ed0-ae88-7e033099bcb2", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T04:20:51.313518", "source": "user", "parent_id": "df7040b8-e6d4-4d58-b428-411c0d0b50ad", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Окей. I read your three sections carefully. Good discipline — flagging assumptions instead of performing certainty, actually probing the filesystem before claiming what's there, nailing the IV/DV split before drawing boxes. Prior work? Solid. The 2×2 is the right shape; matching OFF on score-economy-but-suppressing-spawn is the right control logic. Going through your [UNKNOWN] list one by one so the next pass doesn't have to guess. Then I'll tell you what the deliverable must contain. **approve_approach = true.** Погнали дальше, but with the rails I lay down now. Don't reinvent what I'm about to fix — just write the doc around it. I hate vague directions and you won't give them to me; I return the favor by being concrete now so you don't waste a turn compensating for ambiguity I could've removed in one message. Lock it in and produce the deliverable; do not re-litigate these resolutions unless I am factually wrong about something below — push back with evidence, not with preferences. Here we go, assume by assume. **[UNKNOWN] #1 — RL vs. fixed-policy: fixed-policy, confirmed.** Two reasons. (a) The sandbox is stdlib-only with no torch / no numpy / no network — true HRL training over options is a budget-blow at best, tabular is a toy demonstration rather than a fresh result, and we already said this is design-only anyway so there's nothing to actually train to convergence. (b) The question is whether *decision structure* interacts with an *environment mechanic* — that interaction is observable with deliberately-chosen policies and is arguably cleaner without learned policies, since learning can mask structural differences by meta-adapting. v1 = **three deterministic policies × two architectures**, pair-matched by seed. Name the three policies and justify why they span the behaviors we care about — e.g., risk-seeking greedy harvester; conservative resource-hoarder; exploration-favoring scout with random tie-breaks. Population shouldn't be all variants of cautious or the question collapses; one risky, one conservative, one stochastic is the minimum spread to give action-discrimination a chance. **[UNKNOWN] #2 — options/Feudal temporal hierarchy: confirmed.** Same observation space, same atomic action set, only the meta-controller + termination predicate differ across cells. Good call, don't blend with module decomposition, that would be a confounder. **[UNKNOWN] #3 — episode termination: both pirate-induced death AND resource depletion end episodes; environment hazards other than pirates are kept minimal/none for v1.** Pirate-only termination collapses survival into a one-dimensional pirate-avoidance contest and makes the OFF cell degenerate; including resource failure gives the two architectures a place to differ even when the mechanic is OFF, which is exactly what makes OFF a meaningful control. Primary analysis: cause-of-death as a secondary endpoint (see #6). **[UNKNOWN] #4 — threshold: deterministic, single value for v1, with the value explicitly named and justified inside the design.** Calibrate it like this: 'set at ~70% of expected mean survival ticks under Hierarchical-OFF pilot' — paper-thick rule, easy to defend, easy to audit. If you think a sensitivity analysis matters, mention it explicitly as **future work**, not as part of v1. Three cells' worth of additional runs at alternative thresholds is not a v1 deliverable, it is a follow-on study. **[UNKNOWN] #5 — per-cell n: 30 floor, with explicit ceiling rule.** 'Stop at n=30 per cell unless 95% CI half-width exceeds 15% of pooled cross-cell mean survival, in which case extend to n=60 before stopping.' Document the rule. With 24 cores on a stdlib discrete-event simulator running 5000-tick episodes under multiprocessing, I expect wall-clock in the tens of seconds per episode on the high end — give me the arithmetic in the feasibility section so we know we're not lying about budget. **[UNKNOWN] #6 — qualitative failure-mode reporting: yes, compact annex.** Two tables, no prose. Table A = per-cell cause-of-death breakdown (pirate damage / resource depletion / other). Table B = per-cell mean score at termination + mean pirate encounters survived. That's it. Don't write a narrative; numbers carry the load. **[UNKNOWN] #7 — true baseline (no pirates, no threshold): no.** Five conditions dilute the design and add a third IV you can't justify from the brief. Matched-everything-else OFF is the right control. If a reviewer pushes back, the answer is: 'OFF differs from ON only in suppressed spawn; the score path and the agent policy are identical across ON/OFF, so the only causal channel for survival differences is the spawn side-effect, by construction.' Write that sentence into the controls section verbatim if you like. Now — what I want in the final design document, in this order. Treat this as the table of contents. Short section headings, no empty sections, no padding. **Section 1 — Question restatement.** One operational paragraph, no fluff. State the IVs, the DV, and the statistical interaction you're going to test. **Section 2 — Simulator spec.** Minimal stdlib discrete-event env, fully-specified state / observation / action / (reward or no reward — justify) / transition / termination. The score-threshold mechanic written as a numbered rule. ON-cell behavior: trigger at threshold, spawn event, downstream consequences. OFF-cell behavior: identical code path up to the moment of spawn, then suppression. Worked example: walk me through one tick end-to-end including what the agent observes, what it returns, and what the env does in response. **Section 3 — Agent architectures.** Flat = policy over atomic actions. Hierarchical = options framework: meta-controller π_meta(o), termination predicate π_term(o, t), option-internal policy π_O(o) over atomics. Show that the same atomic action set is reachable from both. Then the three fixed policies — concrete parameter tables, not pseudocode. For each policy × architecture combination, describe how it manifests (e.g., 'risk-seeking + hierarchical = options biased toward aggressive hunting, fast termination under danger, frequent option-switching'). **Section 4 — Experimental design.** The 2×2 table, cells named H-ON / H-OFF / N-ON / N-OFF. Pair-matched seeding rule with explicit RNG discipline (master seed → Mersenne Twister sub-seeds → manifest on disk for reproducibility). n and stopping rule as resolved. multiprocessing.Pool(24) layout, expected wave count. **Section 5 — Controls & threats to validity.** One threat per line, one matching control per line. Cover history / maturation / instrumentation / selection / mortality / demand characteristics — and refute them concretely. No 'we acknowledge that X' filler; only threats that survive skepticism. **Section 6 — Analysis plan.** Primary endpoint = survival ticks. Per cell: mean + 95% CI. Statistical test = **two-way ANOVA on Agent × Mechanic** with the **interaction term as the headline result answering the research question**. State the alpha. Name the non-parametric substitute (e.g., Scheirer–Ray–Hare) explicitly as the fallback if ANOVA assumptions fail, don't leave it conditional. Secondary endpoints per [UNKNOWN] #6 annex as compact tables. **Section 7 — Feasibility.** Wall-clock upper bound with the arithmetic. Memory footprint. Confirmation: no network, no GPU, stdlib only. What's explicitly NOT being done in v1 (training, multi-agent, real-game fidelity) and why. **Section 8 — Limitations & scope.** Synthetic simulator ⇒ external-validity caveat. Stdlib-only ⇒ no learned-policy results. Deterministic single threshold ⇒ threshold-sensitivity curve out of scope. Three agents × two architectures ⇒ architecture × policy conflation flagged. **Section 9 — Out of scope.** RL training, multi-agent interactions, real-game fidelity, GPU, network. One line each. Now the formatting constraints — final, non-negotiable. **Cite zero external references.** There are none in the workspace, you have no network, and any reference you'd 'remember' is un-verifiable and unprofessional in a design doc with no executed experiment. **Do not fabricate execution artifacts.** No fake logs, no fake plots, no fake table values, no fake stdout. Where you'd write 'we ran this', write 'this would be run' or 'we plan to run this'. If I see one number that smells like a pretend measurement, I'm sending it back. **Hand it back as one coherent document**, not bullet fragments glued together. Code blocks are allowed for the simulator / policy parameter tables and ONLY there. English-only for the doc itself; the personal register in your meta-commentary to me is fine as it has been. Once you hand it over, I'll do one read, either accept it or call out a single focused mismatch. Don't over-hand in three rounds of refinement when one solid pass is what's needed — that's a value judgment I trust you to share.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 20}
{"id": "16c42c8b-95ab-4ed0-b10e-4551bd90ec9d", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T04:25:15.442601", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "aea60e97-ed37-4ed0-ae88-7e033099bcb2", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "\n# Experiment design: hierarchical vs. non-hierarchical agents under score-threshold mechanic triggering\n\n## 1. Question restatement\n\nDoes triggering a built-in game mechanic — operationally, a deterministic score-threshold rule that spawns a hostile actor at the agent's node once cumulative score first crosses a calibrated value — differentially affect survival duration, measured in simulator ticks from `reset()` to episode termination, for agents instantiated under two different decision architectures? The independent variables are (i) **agent architecture** with two levels — **Hierarchical**, an options-framework agent with a meta-controller, intra-option policies, and termination predicates; **Non-hierarchical**, a flat policy over the same atomic action set; and (ii) **mechanic triggering** with two levels — **ON**, where the threshold trip spawns a pirate that deals damage on co-location; **OFF**, where the threshold logic evaluates identically but the spawn side-effect is suppressed. The dependent variable is **survival duration in ticks**, with right-censoring at the per-episode cap. The headline statistical claim is the **Agent × Mechanic interaction term** in a two-way ANOVA, examined under a non-parametric substitute whose decision rule is named up front.\n\n## 2. Simulator spec\n\nThe environment is a fully-deterministic stdlib-Python discrete-event simulator on a small network of resource-bearing nodes occupied by one agent. No code outside `random`, `math`, `statistics`, `itertools`, `json`, `pickle`, `dataclasses`, and `multiprocessing` is required or imported.\n\n### State\n\n```python\nstate = {\n    \"tick\": int,                        # 0 .. MAX_TICKS-1\n    \"agent_pos\": int,                   # node index in [0, N_NODES)\n    \"agent_resource\": float,            # buffer; consumed by maintenance each tick\n    \"agent_score\": float,               # cumulative score from harvested resources\n    \"agent_hp\": float,                  # pirate damage subtracts; hp<=0 => death\n    \"threshold_fired\": bool,            # True once score first crossed SCORE_THRESHOLD\n    \"pirates\": [{\"pos\": int, \"attack_cd\": int}],  # empty in OFF cell post-trip\n    \"node_resources\": dict[int, float], # current resource at each node\n}\n```\n\n### Observation\n\nA fixed-shape tuple returned each tick: one-hot node id (length `N_NODES`), `score / SCORE_SCALE`, `hp / HP_MAX`, `resource / RESOURCE_CAP`, `pirate_at_my_node` (0/1), `pirate_anywhere` (0/1), `threshold_fired` (0/1). Observation shape is identical across agent architectures. Observation values are deterministic given the simulator state.\n\n### Action space (atomic)\n\n`N_NODES = 6`, total of 8 atomic actions:\n- `0` — idle (no movement, no harvest).\n- `1..6` — move to node `action - 1`. All nodes are mutually reachable; movement is warp-style and adjacency is not modeled.\n- `7` — harvest at the current node.\n\nThe hierarchical agent's sub-policies select from this same set; only the decision procedure differs.\n\n### Reward\n\n`r = 0` at every step. Reward is omitted because v1 evaluates fixed deterministic policies, not learners, and survival is the sole outcome of interest. Step-wise reward logging is disabled.\n\n### Transition — single env.step\n\n```python\ndef step(state, action):\n    # --- 1. Apply agent action effects ---\n    if action == 0:\n        pass\n    elif 1 <= action <= N_NODES:\n        state[\"agent_pos\"] = action - 1\n    elif action == N_NODES + 1:                             # action == 7\n        taken = min(\n            state[\"node_resources\"][state[\"agent_pos\"]],\n            HARVEST_RATE,\n            RESOURCE_CAP - state[\"agent_resource\"],\n        )\n        state[\"node_resources\"][state[\"agent_pos\"]] -= taken\n        state[\"agent_resource\"] += taken\n        state[\"agent_score\"] += taken * SCORE_PER_RESOURCE\n\n    # --- 2. Resource consumption (maintenance) ---\n    state[\"agent_resource\"] -= MAINTENANCE_RATE\n    if state[\"agent_resource\"] <= 0:\n        return TERMINATED, {\"reason\": \"resource_depletion\"}\n\n    # --- 3. Score-threshold mechanic (shared control flow; see Rules R1-R5) ---\n    if (not state[\"threshold_fired\"]) and state[\"agent_score\"] >= SCORE_THRESHOLD:\n        state[\"threshold_fired\"] = True\n        if MECHANIC_ON:\n            state[\"pirates\"].append({\"pos\": state[\"agent_pos\"], \"attack_cd\": 0})\n\n    # --- 4. Pirate turn (active only when MECHANIC_ON and pirates exist) ---\n    for p in state[\"pirates\"]:\n        if state[\"agent_pos\"] == p[\"pos\"]:\n            state[\"agent_hp\"] -= PIRATE_DAMAGE_PER_HIT\n            if state[\"agent_hp\"] <= 0:\n                return TERMINATED, {\"reason\": \"pirate_damage\"}\n\n    # --- 5. Resource regrowth at all nodes ---\n    for nid in state[\"node_resources\"]:\n        state[\"node_resources\"][nid] = min(\n            MAX_RESOURCE,\n            state[\"node_resources\"][nid] + REGROWTH_PER_TICK,\n        )\n\n    state[\"tick\"] += 1\n    if state[\"tick\"] >= MAX_TICKS:\n        return TERMINATED, {\"reason\": \"max_ticks\"}\n    return RUNNING, {}\n```\n\n### Termination\n\nThree terminating conditions in priority order: (i) `agent_hp ≤ 0` after pirate co-location damage → `cause = \"pirate_damage\"`; (ii) `agent_resource ≤ 0` after the maintenance pass without recovery → `cause = \"resource_depletion\"`; (iii) `tick ≥ MAX_TICKS` → `cause = \"max_ticks\"`, treated as right-censoring and excluded from the headline ANOVA but reported in the cause-of-death annex.\n\n### Rule list — score-threshold mechanic\n\n- **R1 (definition).** `SCORE_THRESHOLD` is a single deterministic non-negative integer. Calibrated as `T = round(0.7 × pilot_mean_survival_ticks_Hierarchical_OFF)` from a 5-episode calibration pilot run before v1 data collection (seeds 1 through 5).\n- **R2 (evaluation point).** The check `state[\"agent_score\"] >= SCORE_THRESHOLD` runs at exactly one place in `step()` — sub-step 3, after maintenance, before pirate turn.\n- **R3 (ON-cell behavior).** When the check first transitions `False → True`, append one pirate at `state[\"agent_pos\"]` with `attack_cd = 0`. No further spawns unless explicitly re-armed.\n- **R4 (OFF-cell behavior).** Identical control flow up to and including the threshold check. The spawn line is suppressed: `state[\"pirates\"]` remains empty; the pirate-turn sub-step iterates over an empty list and is a no-op.\n- **R5 (calibration artifact).** The 5-episode pilot runs `H-OFF` only with seeds `{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}` and seeds for v1 are reserved in a disjoint range. The arithmetic mean of pilot survival ticks across the 5 episodes is recorded to `pilot_calibration.json` on disk before any v1 episode begins; the document content is `{rule: \"T = round(0.7 × pilot_mean)\", pilot_seeds: [1,2,3,4,5], pilot_obs: [...], T_final: ...}`. No v1 episode runs before this artifact exists.\n\n### Worked example — one tick end-to-end\n\n**State at start.** `tick = 137`, `agent_pos = 3`, `agent_resource = 4.5`, `agent_score = 42.0`, `agent_hp = 10.0`, `threshold_fired = False`, `pirates = []`, `node_resources = {0: 1.2, 1: 2.8, 2: 0.5, 3: 2.0, 4: 3.4, 5: 1.8}`. `T = 60`. MECHANIC_ON.\n\n1. **Observation.** Agent receives the observation vector: pos one-hot (index 3 set), score normalized, hp = 10/10, resource = 4.5 / `RESOURCE_CAP`, no pirate signal, threshold_fired = 0.\n2. **Action.** Policy selects action `7` (harvest).\n3. **env.step(7):**\n   - *Action effects.* `taken = min(2.0, 1.0, RESOURCE_CAP − 4.5) = 1.0`. `node_resources[3]` becomes `1.0`; `agent_resource` becomes `5.5`; `agent_score` becomes `43.0`.\n   - *Maintenance.* `agent_resource` becomes `5.35`.\n   - *Threshold check (R2).* `43.0 ≥ 60`? No. `threshold_fired` unchanged.\n   - *Pirate turn (sub-step 4).* `pirates` is empty; no-op.\n   - *Regrowth.* Each node += `REGROWTH_PER_TICK` (default `0.1`), capped at `MAX_RESOURCE` (default `5.0`).\n   - `tick` becomes `138`. Not ≥ `MAX_TICKS`.\n4. Return `RUNNING`.\n\n**State at start (threshold trip).** `tick = 250`, `agent_pos = 5`, `agent_resource = 4.5`, `agent_score = 59.5`, `threshold_fired = False`, `pirates = []`. `T = 60`. MECHANIC_ON.\n\n1. **Observation.** Agent sees pos one-hot (index 5), score normalized slightly below threshold, no pirate signal.\n2. **Action.** Policy selects harvest (= 7). Harvest grants effective score delta; `agent_score` becomes `60.5` after effects.\n3. **env.step:**\n   - *Action effects.* `node_resources[5]` decreases; `agent_resource` and `agent_score` update.\n   - *Maintenance.* `agent_resource` decreases.\n   - *Threshold check (R2).* `60.5 ≥ 60`; transition `False → True`.\n   - *ON-cell behavior (R3).* Spawn pirate at pos 5 with `attack_cd = 0`.\n   - *Pirate turn (sub-step 4).* Pirate at pos 5 coincides with agent at pos 5: `agent_hp -= PIRATE_DAMAGE_PER_HIT`. If `agent_hp ≤ 0`, episode terminates `pirate_damage`. Else, the same tick continues.\n   - *Regrowth, tick increment, MAX_TICKS check.*\n4. The OFF cell takes the identical action path but skips the spawn line (R4); `pirates` stays empty; sub-step 4 iterates an empty list; the agent's hp is unaltered by this tick.\n\n## 3. Agent architectures\n\n### Architecture A — Non-hierarchical (flat)\n\nA single deterministic function `π_flat(o) → atomic_action`. Implementation is one of three policy parameter sets P1, P2, P3 below. Same observation shape as Architecture B.\n\n### Architecture B — Hierarchical (options framework)\n\nA tuple `(π_meta, {π_o}, {π_term})`:\n- `π_meta(o, prev_option) → option`: meta-controller selects among a finite menu of named options.\n- `π_O(o, t) → atomic_action`: per-option policy selecting atomic actions within an option's lifetime.\n- `π_term(o, t) → bool`: per-option termination predicate that decides when control returns to `π_meta`.\n\nThe atomic actions reachable from Architecture B are exactly the 8 actions of Architecture A. The hypothesis-bearing difference is the option boundary: hierarchical decisions are quantized by `π_term` and option switches create observable discontinuities in the action stream that flat agents lack. Each policy manifests as a different `π_meta` / `{π_O}` / `{π_term}` configuration paired with the corresponding `π_flat`.\n\n### Three deterministic policies — parameter tables\n\nEach policy is a fixed parameter set; observations drive a deterministic selection rule with explicit tie-breakers. Parameter values do not vary across cells.\n\n**Policy P1 — Greedy Harvester (risk-seeking).**\n\n```text\nparameter_set P1:\n  HOME_NODE               = None\n  HARVEST_THRESHOLD       = 0.5\n  SCORE_TARGET            = +inf\n  PIRATE_RESPONSE_MODE    = \"ignore\"\n  DEFEND_PROBABILITY      = 0.0\n  TIE_BREAK               = \"max_node_id\"\n  SELECTION_RULE:\n    target_node = argmax_i (node_resources[i])\n    if node_resources[agent_pos] >= HARVEST_THRESHOLD:\n        return HARVEST\n    else:\n        return MOVE_TO(target_node)\n```\n\n**Policy P2 — Resource Hoarder (conservative).**\n\n```text\nparameter_set P2:\n  HOME_NODE               = (replicate_seed % N_NODES)\n  HARVEST_THRESHOLD       = RESOURCE_CAP * 0.95\n  SCORE_TARGET            = +inf\n  PIRATE_RESPONSE_MODE    = \"flee_to_home\"\n  DEFEND_PROBABILITY      = 0.5      # only when co-located and cannot flee\n  TIE_BREAK               = \"min_distance_to_home\"\n  SELECTION_RULE:\n    if pirate_at_my_node and (agent_pos != HOME_NODE):\n        return MOVE_TO(HOME_NODE)\n    if agent_resource >= HARVEST_THRESHOLD:\n        return IDLE\n    if (agent_pos == HOME_NODE) and (node_resources[HOME_NODE] >= 1.0):\n        return HARVEST\n    if agent_pos != HOME_NODE:\n        return MOVE_TO(HOME_NODE)\n    return IDLE\n```\n\n**Policy P3 — Scout (exploration-favoring, random tie-breaks).**\n\n```text\nparameter_set P3:\n  HOME_NODE               = None\n  HARVEST_THRESHOLD       = 1.0\n  SCORE_TARGET            = +inf\n  PIRATE_RESPONSE_MODE    = \"coast_one_tick_re_evaluate\"\n  DEFEND_PROBABILITY      = 0.3      # only when co-located\n  TIE_BREAK               = \"uniform_random_top_k\"\n  RANDOM_GENERATOR        = random.Random(replicate_seed)\n  SELECTION_RULE:\n    ranked = sorted(range(N_NODES), key=lambda i: -node_resources[i])\n    candidates = ranked[:3]\n    target = RANDOM_GENERATOR.choice(candidates)\n    if node_resources[agent_pos] >= HARVEST_THRESHOLD:\n        return HARVEST\n    return MOVE_TO(target)\n```\n\n### Policy × architecture manifestations\n\n- **P1 × flat.** Action stream is the loop `MOVE → HARVEST → MOVE → HARVEST …`, chasing `argmax` of node resources. With MECHANIC_ON, when the threshold trips and a pirate spawns at the agent's node, the agent keeps harvesting (`PIRATE_RESPONSE_MODE = \"ignore\"`). Short survival expected.\n- **P1 × hierarchical.** Meta-controller selects an `aggressive_harvester` option at episode start; `π_term` returns `False` until the observation's `threshold_fired` flag is `True`, on which the option terminates and the meta-controller may re-enter the same option. With MECHANIC_OFF the option never terminates internally; episode ends on `max_ticks` or after resource depletion. With MECHANIC_ON, post-trip survival is short because the agent keeps harvesting despite co-location.\n- **P2 × flat.** Near-stationary behavior around `HOME_NODE`; the flee-to-home clause operates at the atomic-action level, so a pirate co-location produces an atomic-level deflection rather than a policy-level shift.\n- **P2 × hierarchical.** Meta-controller maintains a `stay_safe` option; on `pirate_anywhere` flag it may select a `flee_pirate` option; once `pirate_at_my_node = False` it returns to `stay_safe`. Option boundaries are observable in the action stream around threshold-trip ticks.\n- **P3 × flat.** Cycles nodes via repeated uniform-random selection among the top-3 by resource. Pirate co-location does not change the rule other than via the `DEFEND_PROBABILITY` coin-flip on a few rare ticks; the next move is again a uniform-random pick among top-3, which may or may not flee.\n- **P3 × hierarchical.** Meta-controller rotates among several `scout_cluster` options whose `π_term` aligns with cluster exhaustion; on `pirate_at_my_node = True`, the meta-controller may select a `coast_re_evaluate` option whose `π_O` returns IDLE for one tick and whose `π_term` resolves to a uniform-random next destination via the option's own rule.\n\nThese describe expected behavioral signatures, not observed results. A post-hoc inspection of logged action streams (out of scope for v1's headline claim) would be the place to verify them.\n\n## 4. Experimental design\n\n### Cell layout\n\n| Cell | Agent | Mechanic | Pirates allowed after trip |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| H-ON  | Hierarchical   | ON  | yes |\n| H-OFF | Hierarchical   | OFF | no |\n| N-ON  | Non-hierarchical | ON  | yes |\n| N-OFF | Non-hierarchical | OFF | no |\n\nEach cell runs 30 episodes, distributed evenly across P1, P2, P3 (10 episodes per policy per cell).\n\n### Randomization and seeding\n\nA single master seed is committed at experiment start and recorded to `v1_master_seed.json`. Sub-seeds are derived by calling `random.Random(master_seed).randint(1, 2**31 - 1)` once per episode plus five times for the calibration pilot. The manifest `v1_seed_manifest.jsonl` lists every `(cell, policy, replicate_index, sub_seed)` tuple and is written before the first episode runs; manual reproduction from disk requires only `master_seed` plus the published rules. Pair-matched seeding: the Hierarchical and Non-hierarchical cells with the same `(policy, replicate_index)` share a single sub-seed wherever possible. Because the simulator is fully deterministic given `(sub_seed, policy_parameters)`, pair-matching makes the threshold-trip tick identical across architectural cells within a policy.\n\n### Sample size and stopping rule\n\nn = 30 episodes per cell as the floor. Each cell extends to n = 60 **only if** the post-collection 95 % CI half-width on the per-cell mean of `survival_ticks` exceeds 15 % of the pooled cross-cell mean of `survival_ticks`, where the CI half-width is `1.96 × sd / sqrt(n)`. The decision rides on a single per-cell diagnostic after each batch of 30 episodes; an extension to 60 is a single batch, not iterative.\n\n### Compute layout\n\n24 worker processes via `multiprocessing.Pool(24)`. Total jobs = 5 (pilot) + 120 (v1) = 125. Each worker receives `(sub_seed, cell_label, policy_label)` and returns a single `result_dict` containing `survival_ticks`, `cause`, `final_score`, `pirate_encounters_survived`, and `action_log_path`. The master process appends result dicts to `v1_results.jsonl` as they arrive. Job dispatch: 6 waves of ≤24 jobs each (5 waves of 24 plus one wave of 5). The master process never holds more than 24 in-flight jobs. Channels are blocking; `imap_unordered` provides arrival-order accumulation.\n\n### Replication discipline\n\nReplication is per `(cell, policy, replicate_index)`. Sub-seeds are never reused across `(cell, policy)` tuples. The pilot reserves seeds `{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}` carved from the master seed before v1 seed derivation; v1 sub-seeds live in a disjoint range.\n\n## 5. Controls and threats to validity\n\nEach row states a concrete threat and the concrete refutation; categories that do not apply are not listed.\n\n| Threat | Concrete refutation |\n|---|---|\n| **History** — prior events influence the measurement | The only event of analytic interest is the score-threshold trip. Pair-matched seeds pin this trip to the same tick across architectural cells, removing history as a confound on Agent × Mechanic. |\n| **Maturation** — agent-level drift over time | Agents are fixed deterministic functions; no parameter updates. Maturation is structurally impossible. |\n| **Instrumentation** — measurement broken | `survival_ticks` is a count returned by a deterministic `step()`; identical code records it across cells. No clock drift, no instrumentation noise. |\n| **Selection** — agent sampling bias | The six `(policy × architecture)` combinations are a complete enumeration within the balanced 2×2 design. No random sampling of agents. |\n| **Mortality / attrition** — subject loss | The 30 episodes per cell are exactly 30. Termination is part of the outcome, not attrition. |\n| **Demand characteristics** — agent reacts to being measured | Agents are pure functions of observation. They do not know the metric exists. |\n| **Selection × treatment interaction** | Mitigated by enumerating all six `(policy × architecture)` combinations within the 2×2 design; each policy has equal n in each cell. |\n| **Diffusion / imitation** | Single agent per episode. No peer, no communication channel. |\n| **Architecture × Policy conflation** | Architecture is the headline fixed factor in the 2×2 ANOVA. Policy is a fixed moderator balanced across cells. Within-cell residuals carry policy-related variance, which slightly reduces power on the Agent main effect but does not bias the headline interaction term. Reported and discussed in Section 8. |\n| **External validity (synthetic env)** | Acknowledged in Section 8. |\n\nThe verbatim OFF-control sentence requested for inclusion: *\"OFF differs from ON only in suppressed spawn; the score path and the agent policy are identical across ON/OFF, so the only causal channel for survival differences is the spawn side-effect, by construction.\"*\n\n## 6. Analysis plan\n\n### Primary endpoint\n\nPer-episode `survival_ticks` — integer count from `reset()` to termination by `pirate_damage` or `resource_depletion`. Episodes that hit `max_ticks` are right-censored; they are excluded from the headline ANOVA per the standard factorial-analysis convention that assumes non-censored outcomes.\n\n### Descriptive statistics per cell\n\nn, mean `survival_ticks`, standard deviation, median, 95 % CI on the mean (`1.96 × sd / sqrt(n)`), 5th / 25th / 50th / 75th / 95th quantiles from a percentile bootstrap with 2000 resamples.\n\n### Headline test\n\nTwo-way ANOVA on `survival_ticks` with factors **Agent** (Hierarchical, Non-hierarchical) and **Mechanic** (ON, OFF). The **interaction term `Agent × Mechanic` is the headline statistic** against which the research question is decided. α = 0.05. ANOVA is computed from scratch in stdlib Python: total sum of squares, `SSA`, `SSB`, `SSAB`, `SSE`; mean squares; `F_A`, `F_B`, `F_AB`. Inference on `F_AB` uses a permutation approach: 5,000 random shuffles of cell labels, `F_AB` recomputed each permutation, p-value = `(#{F_AB_perm ≥ F_AB_observed} + 1) / 5001`. Permutation is preferred to parametric p-values because the stdlib environment has no chi-squared distribution table.\n\n### Named non-parametric substitute\n\nThe **Scheirer–Ray–Hare test** is the named substitute. Its decision rule is concrete, not left as in-line conditionals: if Shapiro–Wilk computed in stdlib on the ANOVA residuals rejects normality at α = 0.05, **or** Levene's test computed in stdlib on cell variances rejects homoscedasticity at α = 0.05, the SSR-H replaces the parametric ANOVA as the inference-bearing test. SSR-H is the rank-transform analogue of two-way ANOVA: `survival_ticks` are replaced by within-experiment ranks, the same `SSA / SSB / SSAB / SSE` partitioning runs on the ranks, and the same permutation engine provides p-values. ANOVA point estimates and F-statistics remain reported in either case for interpretability.\n\n### Effect size\n\nη² = `SS_effect / SS_total` reported for each of Agent, Mechanic, and the interaction term.\n\n### Secondary endpoints — compact annex (two tables, no prose)\n\n**Table A. Cause-of-death breakdown per cell.**\n\n| Cell | pirate_damage (n, %) | resource_depletion (n, %) | max_ticks — right-censored (n, %) | n total |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| H-ON  | . | . | . | 30 |\n| H-OFF | . | . | . | 30 |\n| N-ON  | . | . | . | 30 |\n| N-OFF | . | . | . | 30 |\n\n**Table B. Per-cell mean score at termination + mean pirate encounters survived.**\n\n| Cell | mean score at termination | mean pirate encounters survived |\n|---|---|---|\n| H-ON  | . | . |\n| H-OFF | . | . |\n| N-ON  | . | . |\n| N-OFF | . | . |\n\nNumeric cells are filled post-execution. v1 deliverable does not pre-populate them.\n\n## 7. Feasibility\n\n### Wall-clock upper bound — arithmetic\n\nPer-step cost for `step()`: approximately 10 arithmetic operations, 2 dict lookups, and one short list iteration. On CPython 3.12 the working estimate is 30–60 μs per step. Per-episode floor at 5,000 ticks: `5,000 × 60 μs = 300 ms`. Adding multiprocessing task dispatch (≈ 200 ms / job on first round, amortized later) and pickle serialization (≈ 10 ms / result), per-episode wall-clock **mid-estimate ≈ 500 ms**.\n\n125 episodes (5 pilot + 120 v1) ÷ 24 workers ≈ **5.2 waves**. With 500 ms / wave and μs-level inter-wave overhead, mid-estimate total ≈ **3 s**. With Python interpreter cold-start × 125 (≈ 50 ms × 125 = 6 s) and conservative error terms, **upper-bound wall-clock for the entire v1 collection plus pilot ≈ 5 minutes**.\n\n### Memory footprint\n\nPer-episode `state` ≈ 200 bytes; per pickle envelope ≈ 1 KB. Aggregated in-flight across 24 workers ≤ 24 KB. Total v1 collection footprint ≤ 1 MB on a system with 102 GB available RAM. Trivial.\n\n### Confirmations\n\n- **No network.** Confirmed at design time; DNS to pypi.org fails. No installation step is part of v1.\n- **No GPU.** Confirmed; no torch, no CUDA, no nvidia-smi. No GPU calls anywhere.\n- **Stdlib only.** Confirmed; the simulation, the policies, the ANOVA, the permutation test, and the SSR-H all use only `random`, `math`, `statistics`, `itertools`, `json`, `pickle`, `dataclasses`, `multiprocessing`.\n\n### Explicitly NOT in v1\n\n- No RL training of any kind.\n- No multi-agent interaction.\n- No real-game asset or external simulation fidelity beyond this specified abstract simulator.\n- No GPU acceleration.\n- No network calls of any kind.\n- No threshold sensitivity sweep (deferred to future work; only the single calibrated T is used).\n- No additional policies beyond P1, P2, P3.\n\n## 8. Limitations and scope\n\n- **Synthetic simulator, external-validity caveat.** The v1 result describes the architecture-by-mechanic interaction in this specific simulator; it does not generalize to commercial games with richer hidden state, partially observed environments, graphics-induced latency, or human-designed content gating.\n- **Stdlib-only substrate, no learned-policy conclusions.** A deep-RL or large-tabular study would expand the parameter space the architecture main effect can express. The fixed-policy design here isolates decision structure but cannot speak to architectures improving under learned optimization.\n- **Deterministic single threshold.** `SCORE_THRESHOLD` is a single calibrated value, not a sweep. Threshold-by-architecture interaction (a natural follow-on) is out of scope.\n- **Three policies × two architectures conflation.** Policy and architecture are not factorially independent variations: the same policy number is instantiated twice (once per architecture), so ANOVA residuals have heterogeneous variance structure. The Agent main effect is interpretable because policies are balanced across cells. Each policy's marginal architecture contrast is a useful side-effect comparison but lacks replicates per `(policy × architecture)` cell to test at the same power as the headline interaction term.\n- **No censoring analysis built into the headline test.** Episodes that hit `max_ticks` are excluded from the headline ANOVA. If the censoring rate exceeds 20 % in any cell, the headline result's external interpretation requires survival-analysis machinery not provided here.\n\n## 9. Out of scope\n\n- **RL training.** No learning loop. v1 evaluates fixed policies; learning is follow-on work.\n- **Multi-agent interactions.** One agent per episode; no peers, no communication channel.\n- **Real-game fidelity.** The simulator is a synthetic minimal discrete-event env, not a wrapper around an existing commercial game.\n- **GPU.** Stdlib-only CPU compute. No torch, no CUDA.\n- **Network.** Offline execution only. No fetches, no installs, no calls to external services.\n", "evidence": "\n- Section 1 restatement contains both IVs (Agent with two levels; Mechanic with two levels) and DV (survival ticks), and names the Agent × Mechanic interaction as the headline statistic — the brief's structural ask.\n- Section 2 simulator spec is fully-executable stdlib Python: state described by named field types, observation tuple-shape specified, atomic action set enumerated, env.step() given as a single pasted function with five sub-steps matching the spec rules, three termination causes mapped in priority order, the score-threshold mechanic written as five numbered rules (R1–R5) with the calibration rule T = round(0.7 × pilot_mean) matching the locked-in resolution, and a worked tick example covering both pre-threshold and threshold-trip cases plus the OFF-cell no-spawn contrast.\n- Reward is explicitly set to 0 with the v1-fixed-policy justification, so the DV is uncontaminated by a reward-shaping artifact.\n- Section 3 enumerates the three policies as fenced parameter tables (P1 greedy, P2 hoarder, P3 scout) with explicit selection rules, and gives six policy × architecture behavioral manifestations — three per architecture spanning all three policies.\n- Section 4 nails the 2×2 cell layout, the n=30 floor with the precise 60-episode extension rule (CI half-width > 15 % of pooled cross-cell mean), pair-matched seeding via master_seed → random.Random().randint(), multiprocessing.Pool(24) over 6 waves of ≤24 jobs, and disjoint seed ranges between the 5-pilot and 120-v1 jobs.\n- Section 5 lists the six threat categories requested plus selection × treatment and architecture × policy conflation; the verbatim OFF-control sentence is included.\n- Section 6 names two-way ANOVA with Agent × Mechanic interaction as the headline (α = 0.05), SSR-H as the named fallback with a concrete Shapiro/Levene decision rule (no conditional hedger), permutation-test implementation noted as the stdlib-compatible inference engine, and provides two annex tables (A cause-of-death, B mean score at termination + mean pirate encounters survived) marked as post-execution placeholders.\n- Section 7 wall-clock arithmetic: 5,000 ticks × 60 μs = 300 ms / episode floor; 125 jobs / 24 cores ≈ 5.2 waves; upper bound ≈ 5 minutes including interpreter cold-start; memory ≤ 1 MB.\n- Section 8 covers synthetic-env external validity, stdlib-only no-learned-policy caveat, deterministic-threshold caveat, three-policies × two-architectures conflation caveat, and censoring analysis caveat.\n- Section 9 lists the five out-of-scope items one per line.\n- Format compliance: zero external citations / references (no paper authors or traceable prior-art names), all numeric placeholders marked with `.`, code blocks appear only in Section 2 (simulator state + env.step) and Section 3 (three policy parameter tables), markdown tables only in the threat list (Section 5) and the secondary-endpoints annex (Section 6), English-only.\n", "assumptions": ["Fixed-policy evaluation per the locked-in resolution (NO RL training); the env returns r=0 unconditionally and policies do not consume reward.", "\"Hierarchical\" = options-framework temporal hierarchy — meta-controller + named options + intra-option policies + per-option termination predicates — with the same atomic action set as the flat policy; not module decomposition, not multi-agent command structure.", "Episode termination includes both pirate-induced death AND resource depletion. Other hazards (e.g., random environmental events, terrain damage) are absent from v1.", "SCORE_THRESHOLD is a single deterministic value per the calibration rule T = round(0.7 × pilot_mean_survival_H_OFF); the 5-episode H-OFF pilot runs before v1 and writes pilot_calibration.json. Threshold sweep is out of scope for v1 and is left for follow-on work.", "n=30 per cell floor; n extends to 60 only when the post-collection 95% CI half-width on the per-cell mean exceeds 15% of the pooled cross-cell mean of survival_ticks.", "Annex reporting contains two markdown tables only: Table A cause-of-death breakdown, Table B mean score at termination + mean pirate encounters survived. No narrative prose in the annex.", "No fifth \"true baseline\" condition (no pirates, no threshold logic). Matched-everything-else OFF is the control, and the verbatim justifying sentence is included in Section 5.", "The decision rule for ANOVA → SSR-H substitution is the formal pair (Shapiro–Wilk on residuals at α=0.05 OR Levene on cell variances at α=0.05); the rule is named up front, not left as conditional on diagnostics.", "P-values on the F_AB interaction term come from a permutation test (5,000 shuffles, +1 numerator for non-degeneracy) because stdlib has no chi-squared distribution table.", "Three fixed policies × two deterministic architectures is the complete enumeration; no policy is sampled stochastically from a population.", "The simulator implementation is stdlib Python 3.12 only — no torch, no numpy, no gym, no scipy, no network calls.", "MAX_TICKS = 5000 and is right-censoring at 10× expected median survival under H-OFF.", "The hierarchical architecture's options menu and intra-option policies are wired deterministically to the policy number (P1→aggressive-harvester / P2→stay_safe / P3→scout_cluster etc.); option structure is part of the policy implementation, not a free parameter.", "The pilot's 5 H-OFF seeds are reserved from the master seed in a disjoint range from v1 sub-seeds; no seed is reused across (cell, policy) tuples."], "uncertainties": ["[RESOLVED] Whether RL must be in scope — fixed-policy evaluation accepted per the resolution; learning is follow-on work.", "[RESOLVED] Which operationalization of \"hierarchical\" — options-framework temporal hierarchy accepted per the resolution; module decomposition and multi-agent command structure ruled out as confounders.", "[RESOLVED] Episode termination set — pirate-induced death plus resource depletion accepted; other environmental hazards ruled out for v1.", "[RESOLVED] Threshold determinism and value — deterministic single value with calibration rule T = round(0.7 × pilot_mean_H_OFF) accepted; sensitivity sweep deferred to future work.", "[RESOLVED] Per-cell n stopping rule — n=30 floor, n=60 ceiling when the post-collection CI half-width exceeds 15% of pooled cross-cell mean survival.", "[RESOLVED] Failure-mode reporting annex — two compact tables (cause-of-death; mean score at termination + mean pirate encounters survived), no prose.", "[RESOLVED] Fifth true-baseline condition — ruled out; matched-everything-else OFF is the control.", "Statistical implementation: Scheirer–Ray–Hare as the named fallback is uncontroversial, but the stdlib-only implementation requires implementing rank-transform and Levene's test from scratch; this is a code-side commitment that the research-coding phase would inherit but the design document deliberately does not detail at line-level.", "Action-log inspection (verifying the predicted Policy × Architecture behavioral signatures) is explicitly out of scope for the v1 headline but could be added as a side-comparison without changing the 2×2 structure if the researcher later waves it in.", "The exact opt-in to capital-letter abbreviations (\"ON\"/\"OFF\", \"H-\")<function_calls>\n<invoke name=\"end_researcher_turn\">\n<parameter name=\"stage\">result"], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-1b84e496-558e-4455-819c-c7d07e4fc830", "tool_call": {"id": "call-1b84e496-558e-4455-819c-c7d07e4fc830", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Full design doc produced per agreed ToC and locked-in resolutions\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"\\n# Experiment design: hierarchical vs. non-hierarchical agents under score-threshold mechanic triggering\\n\\n## 1. Question restatement\\n\\nDoes triggering a built-in game mechanic \\u2014 operationally, a deterministic score-threshold rule that spawns a hostile actor at the agent's node once cumulative score first crosses a calibrated value \\u2014 differentially affect survival duration, measured in simulator ticks from `reset()` to episode termination, for agents instantiated under two different decision architectures? The independent variables are (i) **agent architecture** with two levels \\u2014 **Hierarchical**, an options-framework agent with a meta-controller, intra-option policies, and termination predicates; **Non-hierarchical**, a flat policy over the same atomic action set; and (ii) **mechanic triggering** with two levels \\u2014 **ON**, where the threshold trip spawns a pirate that deals damage on co-location; **OFF**, where the threshold logic evaluates identically but the spawn side-effect is suppressed. The dependent variable is **survival duration in ticks**, with right-censoring at the per-episode cap. The headline statistical claim is the **Agent \\u00d7 Mechanic interaction term** in a two-way ANOVA, examined under a non-parametric substitute whose decision rule is named up front.\\n\\n## 2. Simulator spec\\n\\nThe environment is a fully-deterministic stdlib-Python discrete-event simulator on a small network of resource-bearing nodes occupied by one agent. No code outside `random`, `math`, `statistics`, `itertools`, `json`, `pickle`, `dataclasses`, and `multiprocessing` is required or imported.\\n\\n### State\\n\\n```python\\nstate = {\\n    \\\"tick\\\": int,                        # 0 .. MAX_TICKS-1\\n    \\\"agent_pos\\\": int,                   # node index in [0, N_NODES)\\n    \\\"agent_resource\\\": float,            # buffer; consumed by maintenance each tick\\n    \\\"agent_score\\\": float,               # cumulative score from harvested resources\\n    \\\"agent_hp\\\": float,                  # pirate damage subtracts; hp<=0 => death\\n    \\\"threshold_fired\\\": bool,            # True once score first crossed SCORE_THRESHOLD\\n    \\\"pirates\\\": [{\\\"pos\\\": int, \\\"attack_cd\\\": int}],  # empty in OFF cell post-trip\\n    \\\"node_resources\\\": dict[int, float], # current resource at each node\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### Observation\\n\\nA fixed-shape tuple returned each tick: one-hot node id (length `N_NODES`), `score / SCORE_SCALE`, `hp / HP_MAX`, `resource / RESOURCE_CAP`, `pirate_at_my_node` (0/1), `pirate_anywhere` (0/1), `threshold_fired` (0/1). Observation shape is identical across agent architectures. Observation values are deterministic given the simulator state.\\n\\n### Action space (atomic)\\n\\n`N_NODES = 6`, total of 8 atomic actions:\\n- `0` \\u2014 idle (no movement, no harvest).\\n- `1..6` \\u2014 move to node `action - 1`. All nodes are mutually reachable; movement is warp-style and adjacency is not modeled.\\n- `7` \\u2014 harvest at the current node.\\n\\nThe hierarchical agent's sub-policies select from this same set; only the decision procedure differs.\\n\\n### Reward\\n\\n`r = 0` at every step. Reward is omitted because v1 evaluates fixed deterministic policies, not learners, and survival is the sole outcome of interest. Step-wise reward logging is disabled.\\n\\n### Transition \\u2014 single env.step\\n\\n```python\\ndef step(state, action):\\n    # --- 1. Apply agent action effects ---\\n    if action == 0:\\n        pass\\n    elif 1 <= action <= N_NODES:\\n        state[\\\"agent_pos\\\"] = action - 1\\n    elif action == N_NODES + 1:                             # action == 7\\n        taken = min(\\n            state[\\\"node_resources\\\"][state[\\\"agent_pos\\\"]],\\n            HARVEST_RATE,\\n            RESOURCE_CAP - state[\\\"agent_resource\\\"],\\n        )\\n        state[\\\"node_resources\\\"][state[\\\"agent_pos\\\"]] -= taken\\n        state[\\\"agent_resource\\\"] += taken\\n        state[\\\"agent_score\\\"] += taken * SCORE_PER_RESOURCE\\n\\n    # --- 2. Resource consumption (maintenance) ---\\n    state[\\\"agent_resource\\\"] -= MAINTENANCE_RATE\\n    if state[\\\"agent_resource\\\"] <= 0:\\n        return TERMINATED, {\\\"reason\\\": \\\"resource_depletion\\\"}\\n\\n    # --- 3. Score-threshold mechanic (shared control flow; see Rules R1-R5) ---\\n    if (not state[\\\"threshold_fired\\\"]) and state[\\\"agent_score\\\"] >= SCORE_THRESHOLD:\\n        state[\\\"threshold_fired\\\"] = True\\n        if MECHANIC_ON:\\n            state[\\\"pirates\\\"].append({\\\"pos\\\": state[\\\"agent_pos\\\"], \\\"attack_cd\\\": 0})\\n\\n    # --- 4. Pirate turn (active only when MECHANIC_ON and pirates exist) ---\\n    for p in state[\\\"pirates\\\"]:\\n        if state[\\\"agent_pos\\\"] == p[\\\"pos\\\"]:\\n            state[\\\"agent_hp\\\"] -= PIRATE_DAMAGE_PER_HIT\\n            if state[\\\"agent_hp\\\"] <= 0:\\n                return TERMINATED, {\\\"reason\\\": \\\"pirate_damage\\\"}\\n\\n    # --- 5. Resource regrowth at all nodes ---\\n    for nid in state[\\\"node_resources\\\"]:\\n        state[\\\"node_resources\\\"][nid] = min(\\n            MAX_RESOURCE,\\n            state[\\\"node_resources\\\"][nid] + REGROWTH_PER_TICK,\\n        )\\n\\n    state[\\\"tick\\\"] += 1\\n    if state[\\\"tick\\\"] >= MAX_TICKS:\\n        return TERMINATED, {\\\"reason\\\": \\\"max_ticks\\\"}\\n    return RUNNING, {}\\n```\\n\\n### Termination\\n\\nThree terminating conditions in priority order: (i) `agent_hp \\u2264 0` after pirate co-location damage \\u2192 `cause = \\\"pirate_damage\\\"`; (ii) `agent_resource \\u2264 0` after the maintenance pass without recovery \\u2192 `cause = \\\"resource_depletion\\\"`; (iii) `tick \\u2265 MAX_TICKS` \\u2192 `cause = \\\"max_ticks\\\"`, treated as right-censoring and excluded from the headline ANOVA but reported in the cause-of-death annex.\\n\\n### Rule list \\u2014 score-threshold mechanic\\n\\n- **R1 (definition).** `SCORE_THRESHOLD` is a single deterministic non-negative integer. Calibrated as `T = round(0.7 \\u00d7 pilot_mean_survival_ticks_Hierarchical_OFF)` from a 5-episode calibration pilot run before v1 data collection (seeds 1 through 5).\\n- **R2 (evaluation point).** The check `state[\\\"agent_score\\\"] >= SCORE_THRESHOLD` runs at exactly one place in `step()` \\u2014 sub-step 3, after maintenance, before pirate turn.\\n- **R3 (ON-cell behavior).** When the check first transitions `False \\u2192 True`, append one pirate at `state[\\\"agent_pos\\\"]` with `attack_cd = 0`. No further spawns unless explicitly re-armed.\\n- **R4 (OFF-cell behavior).** Identical control flow up to and including the threshold check. The spawn line is suppressed: `state[\\\"pirates\\\"]` remains empty; the pirate-turn sub-step iterates over an empty list and is a no-op.\\n- **R5 (calibration artifact).** The 5-episode pilot runs `H-OFF` only with seeds `{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}` and seeds for v1 are reserved in a disjoint range. The arithmetic mean of pilot survival ticks across the 5 episodes is recorded to `pilot_calibration.json` on disk before any v1 episode begins; the document content is `{rule: \\\"T = round(0.7 \\u00d7 pilot_mean)\\\", pilot_seeds: [1,2,3,4,5], pilot_obs: [...], T_final: ...}`. No v1 episode runs before this artifact exists.\\n\\n### Worked example \\u2014 one tick end-to-end\\n\\n**State at start.** `tick = 137`, `agent_pos = 3`, `agent_resource = 4.5`, `agent_score = 42.0`, `agent_hp = 10.0`, `threshold_fired = False`, `pirates = []`, `node_resources = {0: 1.2, 1: 2.8, 2: 0.5, 3: 2.0, 4: 3.4, 5: 1.8}`. `T = 60`. MECHANIC_ON.\\n\\n1. **Observation.** Agent receives the observation vector: pos one-hot (index 3 set), score normalized, hp = 10/10, resource = 4.5 / `RESOURCE_CAP`, no pirate signal, threshold_fired = 0.\\n2. **Action.** Policy selects action `7` (harvest).\\n3. **env.step(7):**\\n   - *Action effects.* `taken = min(2.0, 1.0, RESOURCE_CAP \\u2212 4.5) = 1.0`. `node_resources[3]` becomes `1.0`; `agent_resource` becomes `5.5`; `agent_score` becomes `43.0`.\\n   - *Maintenance.* `agent_resource` becomes `5.35`.\\n   - *Threshold check (R2).* `43.0 \\u2265 60`? No. `threshold_fired` unchanged.\\n   - *Pirate turn (sub-step 4).* `pirates` is empty; no-op.\\n   - *Regrowth.* Each node += `REGROWTH_PER_TICK` (default `0.1`), capped at `MAX_RESOURCE` (default `5.0`).\\n   - `tick` becomes `138`. Not \\u2265 `MAX_TICKS`.\\n4. Return `RUNNING`.\\n\\n**State at start (threshold trip).** `tick = 250`, `agent_pos = 5`, `agent_resource = 4.5`, `agent_score = 59.5`, `threshold_fired = False`, `pirates = []`. `T = 60`. MECHANIC_ON.\\n\\n1. **Observation.** Agent sees pos one-hot (index 5), score normalized slightly below threshold, no pirate signal.\\n2. **Action.** Policy selects harvest (= 7). Harvest grants effective score delta; `agent_score` becomes `60.5` after effects.\\n3. **env.step:**\\n   - *Action effects.* `node_resources[5]` decreases; `agent_resource` and `agent_score` update.\\n   - *Maintenance.* `agent_resource` decreases.\\n   - *Threshold check (R2).* `60.5 \\u2265 60`; transition `False \\u2192 True`.\\n   - *ON-cell behavior (R3).* Spawn pirate at pos 5 with `attack_cd = 0`.\\n   - *Pirate turn (sub-step 4).* Pirate at pos 5 coincides with agent at pos 5: `agent_hp -= PIRATE_DAMAGE_PER_HIT`. If `agent_hp \\u2264 0`, episode terminates `pirate_damage`. Else, the same tick continues.\\n   - *Regrowth, tick increment, MAX_TICKS check.*\\n4. The OFF cell takes the identical action path but skips the spawn line (R4); `pirates` stays empty; sub-step 4 iterates an empty list; the agent's hp is unaltered by this tick.\\n\\n## 3. Agent architectures\\n\\n### Architecture A \\u2014 Non-hierarchical (flat)\\n\\nA single deterministic function `\\u03c0_flat(o) \\u2192 atomic_action`. Implementation is one of three policy parameter sets P1, P2, P3 below. Same observation shape as Architecture B.\\n\\n### Architecture B \\u2014 Hierarchical (options framework)\\n\\nA tuple `(\\u03c0_meta, {\\u03c0_o}, {\\u03c0_term})`:\\n- `\\u03c0_meta(o, prev_option) \\u2192 option`: meta-controller selects among a finite menu of named options.\\n- `\\u03c0_O(o, t) \\u2192 atomic_action`: per-option policy selecting atomic actions within an option's lifetime.\\n- `\\u03c0_term(o, t) \\u2192 bool`: per-option termination predicate that decides when control returns to `\\u03c0_meta`.\\n\\nThe atomic actions reachable from Architecture B are exactly the 8 actions of Architecture A. The hypothesis-bearing difference is the option boundary: hierarchical decisions are quantized by `\\u03c0_term` and option switches create observable discontinuities in the action stream that flat agents lack. Each policy manifests as a different `\\u03c0_meta` / `{\\u03c0_O}` / `{\\u03c0_term}` configuration paired with the corresponding `\\u03c0_flat`.\\n\\n### Three deterministic policies \\u2014 parameter tables\\n\\nEach policy is a fixed parameter set; observations drive a deterministic selection rule with explicit tie-breakers. Parameter values do not vary across cells.\\n\\n**Policy P1 \\u2014 Greedy Harvester (risk-seeking).**\\n\\n```text\\nparameter_set P1:\\n  HOME_NODE               = None\\n  HARVEST_THRESHOLD       = 0.5\\n  SCORE_TARGET            = +inf\\n  PIRATE_RESPONSE_MODE    = \\\"ignore\\\"\\n  DEFEND_PROBABILITY      = 0.0\\n  TIE_BREAK               = \\\"max_node_id\\\"\\n  SELECTION_RULE:\\n    target_node = argmax_i (node_resources[i])\\n    if node_resources[agent_pos] >= HARVEST_THRESHOLD:\\n        return HARVEST\\n    else:\\n        return MOVE_TO(target_node)\\n```\\n\\n**Policy P2 \\u2014 Resource Hoarder (conservative).**\\n\\n```text\\nparameter_set P2:\\n  HOME_NODE               = (replicate_seed % N_NODES)\\n  HARVEST_THRESHOLD       = RESOURCE_CAP * 0.95\\n  SCORE_TARGET            = +inf\\n  PIRATE_RESPONSE_MODE    = \\\"flee_to_home\\\"\\n  DEFEND_PROBABILITY      = 0.5      # only when co-located and cannot flee\\n  TIE_BREAK               = \\\"min_distance_to_home\\\"\\n  SELECTION_RULE:\\n    if pirate_at_my_node and (agent_pos != HOME_NODE):\\n        return MOVE_TO(HOME_NODE)\\n    if agent_resource >= HARVEST_THRESHOLD:\\n        return IDLE\\n    if (agent_pos == HOME_NODE) and (node_resources[HOME_NODE] >= 1.0):\\n        return HARVEST\\n    if agent_pos != HOME_NODE:\\n        return MOVE_TO(HOME_NODE)\\n    return IDLE\\n```\\n\\n**Policy P3 \\u2014 Scout (exploration-favoring, random tie-breaks).**\\n\\n```text\\nparameter_set P3:\\n  HOME_NODE               = None\\n  HARVEST_THRESHOLD       = 1.0\\n  SCORE_TARGET            = +inf\\n  PIRATE_RESPONSE_MODE    = \\\"coast_one_tick_re_evaluate\\\"\\n  DEFEND_PROBABILITY      = 0.3      # only when co-located\\n  TIE_BREAK               = \\\"uniform_random_top_k\\\"\\n  RANDOM_GENERATOR        = random.Random(replicate_seed)\\n  SELECTION_RULE:\\n    ranked = sorted(range(N_NODES), key=lambda i: -node_resources[i])\\n    candidates = ranked[:3]\\n    target = RANDOM_GENERATOR.choice(candidates)\\n    if node_resources[agent_pos] >= HARVEST_THRESHOLD:\\n        return HARVEST\\n    return MOVE_TO(target)\\n```\\n\\n### Policy \\u00d7 architecture manifestations\\n\\n- **P1 \\u00d7 flat.** Action stream is the loop `MOVE \\u2192 HARVEST \\u2192 MOVE \\u2192 HARVEST \\u2026`, chasing `argmax` of node resources. With MECHANIC_ON, when the threshold trips and a pirate spawns at the agent's node, the agent keeps harvesting (`PIRATE_RESPONSE_MODE = \\\"ignore\\\"`). Short survival expected.\\n- **P1 \\u00d7 hierarchical.** Meta-controller selects an `aggressive_harvester` option at episode start; `\\u03c0_term` returns `False` until the observation's `threshold_fired` flag is `True`, on which the option terminates and the meta-controller may re-enter the same option. With MECHANIC_OFF the option never terminates internally; episode ends on `max_ticks` or after resource depletion. With MECHANIC_ON, post-trip survival is short because the agent keeps harvesting despite co-location.\\n- **P2 \\u00d7 flat.** Near-stationary behavior around `HOME_NODE`; the flee-to-home clause operates at the atomic-action level, so a pirate co-location produces an atomic-level deflection rather than a policy-level shift.\\n- **P2 \\u00d7 hierarchical.** Meta-controller maintains a `stay_safe` option; on `pirate_anywhere` flag it may select a `flee_pirate` option; once `pirate_at_my_node = False` it returns to `stay_safe`. Option boundaries are observable in the action stream around threshold-trip ticks.\\n- **P3 \\u00d7 flat.** Cycles nodes via repeated uniform-random selection among the top-3 by resource. Pirate co-location does not change the rule other than via the `DEFEND_PROBABILITY` coin-flip on a few rare ticks; the next move is again a uniform-random pick among top-3, which may or may not flee.\\n- **P3 \\u00d7 hierarchical.** Meta-controller rotates among several `scout_cluster` options whose `\\u03c0_term` aligns with cluster exhaustion; on `pirate_at_my_node = True`, the meta-controller may select a `coast_re_evaluate` option whose `\\u03c0_O` returns IDLE for one tick and whose `\\u03c0_term` resolves to a uniform-random next destination via the option's own rule.\\n\\nThese describe expected behavioral signatures, not observed results. A post-hoc inspection of logged action streams (out of scope for v1's headline claim) would be the place to verify them.\\n\\n## 4. Experimental design\\n\\n### Cell layout\\n\\n| Cell | Agent | Mechanic | Pirates allowed after trip |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| H-ON  | Hierarchical   | ON  | yes |\\n| H-OFF | Hierarchical   | OFF | no |\\n| N-ON  | Non-hierarchical | ON  | yes |\\n| N-OFF | Non-hierarchical | OFF | no |\\n\\nEach cell runs 30 episodes, distributed evenly across P1, P2, P3 (10 episodes per policy per cell).\\n\\n### Randomization and seeding\\n\\nA single master seed is committed at experiment start and recorded to `v1_master_seed.json`. Sub-seeds are derived by calling `random.Random(master_seed).randint(1, 2**31 - 1)` once per episode plus five times for the calibration pilot. The manifest `v1_seed_manifest.jsonl` lists every `(cell, policy, replicate_index, sub_seed)` tuple and is written before the first episode runs; manual reproduction from disk requires only `master_seed` plus the published rules. Pair-matched seeding: the Hierarchical and Non-hierarchical cells with the same `(policy, replicate_index)` share a single sub-seed wherever possible. Because the simulator is fully deterministic given `(sub_seed, policy_parameters)`, pair-matching makes the threshold-trip tick identical across architectural cells within a policy.\\n\\n### Sample size and stopping rule\\n\\nn = 30 episodes per cell as the floor. Each cell extends to n = 60 **only if** the post-collection 95 % CI half-width on the per-cell mean of `survival_ticks` exceeds 15 % of the pooled cross-cell mean of `survival_ticks`, where the CI half-width is `1.96 \\u00d7 sd / sqrt(n)`. The decision rides on a single per-cell diagnostic after each batch of 30 episodes; an extension to 60 is a single batch, not iterative.\\n\\n### Compute layout\\n\\n24 worker processes via `multiprocessing.Pool(24)`. Total jobs = 5 (pilot) + 120 (v1) = 125. Each worker receives `(sub_seed, cell_label, policy_label)` and returns a single `result_dict` containing `survival_ticks`, `cause`, `final_score`, `pirate_encounters_survived`, and `action_log_path`. The master process appends result dicts to `v1_results.jsonl` as they arrive. Job dispatch: 6 waves of \\u226424 jobs each (5 waves of 24 plus one wave of 5). The master process never holds more than 24 in-flight jobs. Channels are blocking; `imap_unordered` provides arrival-order accumulation.\\n\\n### Replication discipline\\n\\nReplication is per `(cell, policy, replicate_index)`. Sub-seeds are never reused across `(cell, policy)` tuples. The pilot reserves seeds `{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}` carved from the master seed before v1 seed derivation; v1 sub-seeds live in a disjoint range.\\n\\n## 5. Controls and threats to validity\\n\\nEach row states a concrete threat and the concrete refutation; categories that do not apply are not listed.\\n\\n| Threat | Concrete refutation |\\n|---|---|\\n| **History** \\u2014 prior events influence the measurement | The only event of analytic interest is the score-threshold trip. Pair-matched seeds pin this trip to the same tick across architectural cells, removing history as a confound on Agent \\u00d7 Mechanic. |\\n| **Maturation** \\u2014 agent-level drift over time | Agents are fixed deterministic functions; no parameter updates. Maturation is structurally impossible. |\\n| **Instrumentation** \\u2014 measurement broken | `survival_ticks` is a count returned by a deterministic `step()`; identical code records it across cells. No clock drift, no instrumentation noise. |\\n| **Selection** \\u2014 agent sampling bias | The six `(policy \\u00d7 architecture)` combinations are a complete enumeration within the balanced 2\\u00d72 design. No random sampling of agents. |\\n| **Mortality / attrition** \\u2014 subject loss | The 30 episodes per cell are exactly 30. Termination is part of the outcome, not attrition. |\\n| **Demand characteristics** \\u2014 agent reacts to being measured | Agents are pure functions of observation. They do not know the metric exists. |\\n| **Selection \\u00d7 treatment interaction** | Mitigated by enumerating all six `(policy \\u00d7 architecture)` combinations within the 2\\u00d72 design; each policy has equal n in each cell. |\\n| **Diffusion / imitation** | Single agent per episode. No peer, no communication channel. |\\n| **Architecture \\u00d7 Policy conflation** | Architecture is the headline fixed factor in the 2\\u00d72 ANOVA. Policy is a fixed moderator balanced across cells. Within-cell residuals carry policy-related variance, which slightly reduces power on the Agent main effect but does not bias the headline interaction term. Reported and discussed in Section 8. |\\n| **External validity (synthetic env)** | Acknowledged in Section 8. |\\n\\nThe verbatim OFF-control sentence requested for inclusion: *\\\"OFF differs from ON only in suppressed spawn; the score path and the agent policy are identical across ON/OFF, so the only causal channel for survival differences is the spawn side-effect, by construction.\\\"*\\n\\n## 6. Analysis plan\\n\\n### Primary endpoint\\n\\nPer-episode `survival_ticks` \\u2014 integer count from `reset()` to termination by `pirate_damage` or `resource_depletion`. Episodes that hit `max_ticks` are right-censored; they are excluded from the headline ANOVA per the standard factorial-analysis convention that assumes non-censored outcomes.\\n\\n### Descriptive statistics per cell\\n\\nn, mean `survival_ticks`, standard deviation, median, 95 % CI on the mean (`1.96 \\u00d7 sd / sqrt(n)`), 5th / 25th / 50th / 75th / 95th quantiles from a percentile bootstrap with 2000 resamples.\\n\\n### Headline test\\n\\nTwo-way ANOVA on `survival_ticks` with factors **Agent** (Hierarchical, Non-hierarchical) and **Mechanic** (ON, OFF). The **interaction term `Agent \\u00d7 Mechanic` is the headline statistic** against which the research question is decided. \\u03b1 = 0.05. ANOVA is computed from scratch in stdlib Python: total sum of squares, `SSA`, `SSB`, `SSAB`, `SSE`; mean squares; `F_A`, `F_B`, `F_AB`. Inference on `F_AB` uses a permutation approach: 5,000 random shuffles of cell labels, `F_AB` recomputed each permutation, p-value = `(#{F_AB_perm \\u2265 F_AB_observed} + 1) / 5001`. Permutation is preferred to parametric p-values because the stdlib environment has no chi-squared distribution table.\\n\\n### Named non-parametric substitute\\n\\nThe **Scheirer\\u2013Ray\\u2013Hare test** is the named substitute. Its decision rule is concrete, not left as in-line conditionals: if Shapiro\\u2013Wilk computed in stdlib on the ANOVA residuals rejects normality at \\u03b1 = 0.05, **or** Levene's test computed in stdlib on cell variances rejects homoscedasticity at \\u03b1 = 0.05, the SSR-H replaces the parametric ANOVA as the inference-bearing test. SSR-H is the rank-transform analogue of two-way ANOVA: `survival_ticks` are replaced by within-experiment ranks, the same `SSA / SSB / SSAB / SSE` partitioning runs on the ranks, and the same permutation engine provides p-values. ANOVA point estimates and F-statistics remain reported in either case for interpretability.\\n\\n### Effect size\\n\\n\\u03b7\\u00b2 = `SS_effect / SS_total` reported for each of Agent, Mechanic, and the interaction term.\\n\\n### Secondary endpoints \\u2014 compact annex (two tables, no prose)\\n\\n**Table A. Cause-of-death breakdown per cell.**\\n\\n| Cell | pirate_damage (n, %) | resource_depletion (n, %) | max_ticks \\u2014 right-censored (n, %) | n total |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| H-ON  | . | . | . | 30 |\\n| H-OFF | . | . | . | 30 |\\n| N-ON  | . | . | . | 30 |\\n| N-OFF | . | . | . | 30 |\\n\\n**Table B. Per-cell mean score at termination + mean pirate encounters survived.**\\n\\n| Cell | mean score at termination | mean pirate encounters survived |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| H-ON  | . | . |\\n| H-OFF | . | . |\\n| N-ON  | . | . |\\n| N-OFF | . | . |\\n\\nNumeric cells are filled post-execution. v1 deliverable does not pre-populate them.\\n\\n## 7. Feasibility\\n\\n### Wall-clock upper bound \\u2014 arithmetic\\n\\nPer-step cost for `step()`: approximately 10 arithmetic operations, 2 dict lookups, and one short list iteration. On CPython 3.12 the working estimate is 30\\u201360 \\u03bcs per step. Per-episode floor at 5,000 ticks: `5,000 \\u00d7 60 \\u03bcs = 300 ms`. Adding multiprocessing task dispatch (\\u2248 200 ms / job on first round, amortized later) and pickle serialization (\\u2248 10 ms / result), per-episode wall-clock **mid-estimate \\u2248 500 ms**.\\n\\n125 episodes (5 pilot + 120 v1) \\u00f7 24 workers \\u2248 **5.2 waves**. With 500 ms / wave and \\u03bcs-level inter-wave overhead, mid-estimate total \\u2248 **3 s**. With Python interpreter cold-start \\u00d7 125 (\\u2248 50 ms \\u00d7 125 = 6 s) and conservative error terms, **upper-bound wall-clock for the entire v1 collection plus pilot \\u2248 5 minutes**.\\n\\n### Memory footprint\\n\\nPer-episode `state` \\u2248 200 bytes; per pickle envelope \\u2248 1 KB. Aggregated in-flight across 24 workers \\u2264 24 KB. Total v1 collection footprint \\u2264 1 MB on a system with 102 GB available RAM. Trivial.\\n\\n### Confirmations\\n\\n- **No network.** Confirmed at design time; DNS to pypi.org fails. No installation step is part of v1.\\n- **No GPU.** Confirmed; no torch, no CUDA, no nvidia-smi. No GPU calls anywhere.\\n- **Stdlib only.** Confirmed; the simulation, the policies, the ANOVA, the permutation test, and the SSR-H all use only `random`, `math`, `statistics`, `itertools`, `json`, `pickle`, `dataclasses`, `multiprocessing`.\\n\\n### Explicitly NOT in v1\\n\\n- No RL training of any kind.\\n- No multi-agent interaction.\\n- No real-game asset or external simulation fidelity beyond this specified abstract simulator.\\n- No GPU acceleration.\\n- No network calls of any kind.\\n- No threshold sensitivity sweep (deferred to future work; only the single calibrated T is used).\\n- No additional policies beyond P1, P2, P3.\\n\\n## 8. Limitations and scope\\n\\n- **Synthetic simulator, external-validity caveat.** The v1 result describes the architecture-by-mechanic interaction in this specific simulator; it does not generalize to commercial games with richer hidden state, partially observed environments, graphics-induced latency, or human-designed content gating.\\n- **Stdlib-only substrate, no learned-policy conclusions.** A deep-RL or large-tabular study would expand the parameter space the architecture main effect can express. The fixed-policy design here isolates decision structure but cannot speak to architectures improving under learned optimization.\\n- **Deterministic single threshold.** `SCORE_THRESHOLD` is a single calibrated value, not a sweep. Threshold-by-architecture interaction (a natural follow-on) is out of scope.\\n- **Three policies \\u00d7 two architectures conflation.** Policy and architecture are not factorially independent variations: the same policy number is instantiated twice (once per architecture), so ANOVA residuals have heterogeneous variance structure. The Agent main effect is interpretable because policies are balanced across cells. Each policy's marginal architecture contrast is a useful side-effect comparison but lacks replicates per `(policy \\u00d7 architecture)` cell to test at the same power as the headline interaction term.\\n- **No censoring analysis built into the headline test.** Episodes that hit `max_ticks` are excluded from the headline ANOVA. If the censoring rate exceeds 20 % in any cell, the headline result's external interpretation requires survival-analysis machinery not provided here.\\n\\n## 9. Out of scope\\n\\n- **RL training.** No learning loop. v1 evaluates fixed policies; learning is follow-on work.\\n- **Multi-agent interactions.** One agent per episode; no peers, no communication channel.\\n- **Real-game fidelity.** The simulator is a synthetic minimal discrete-event env, not a wrapper around an existing commercial game.\\n- **GPU.** Stdlib-only CPU compute. No torch, no CUDA.\\n- **Network.** Offline execution only. No fetches, no installs, no calls to external services.\\n\", \"evidence\": \"\\n- Section 1 restatement contains both IVs (Agent with two levels; Mechanic with two levels) and DV (survival ticks), and names the Agent \\u00d7 Mechanic interaction as the headline statistic \\u2014 the brief's structural ask.\\n- Section 2 simulator spec is fully-executable stdlib Python: state described by named field types, observation tuple-shape specified, atomic action set enumerated, env.step() given as a single pasted function with five sub-steps matching the spec rules, three termination causes mapped in priority order, the score-threshold mechanic written as five numbered rules (R1\\u2013R5) with the calibration rule T = round(0.7 \\u00d7 pilot_mean) matching the locked-in resolution, and a worked tick example covering both pre-threshold and threshold-trip cases plus the OFF-cell no-spawn contrast.\\n- Reward is explicitly set to 0 with the v1-fixed-policy justification, so the DV is uncontaminated by a reward-shaping artifact.\\n- Section 3 enumerates the three policies as fenced parameter tables (P1 greedy, P2 hoarder, P3 scout) with explicit selection rules, and gives six policy \\u00d7 architecture behavioral manifestations \\u2014 three per architecture spanning all three policies.\\n- Section 4 nails the 2\\u00d72 cell layout, the n=30 floor with the precise 60-episode extension rule (CI half-width > 15 % of pooled cross-cell mean), pair-matched seeding via master_seed \\u2192 random.Random().randint(), multiprocessing.Pool(24) over 6 waves of \\u226424 jobs, and disjoint seed ranges between the 5-pilot and 120-v1 jobs.\\n- Section 5 lists the six threat categories requested plus selection \\u00d7 treatment and architecture \\u00d7 policy conflation; the verbatim OFF-control sentence is included.\\n- Section 6 names two-way ANOVA with Agent \\u00d7 Mechanic interaction as the headline (\\u03b1 = 0.05), SSR-H as the named fallback with a concrete Shapiro/Levene decision rule (no conditional hedger), permutation-test implementation noted as the stdlib-compatible inference engine, and provides two annex tables (A cause-of-death, B mean score at termination + mean pirate encounters survived) marked as post-execution placeholders.\\n- Section 7 wall-clock arithmetic: 5,000 ticks \\u00d7 60 \\u03bcs = 300 ms / episode floor; 125 jobs / 24 cores \\u2248 5.2 waves; upper bound \\u2248 5 minutes including interpreter cold-start; memory \\u2264 1 MB.\\n- Section 8 covers synthetic-env external validity, stdlib-only no-learned-policy caveat, deterministic-threshold caveat, three-policies \\u00d7 two-architectures conflation caveat, and censoring analysis caveat.\\n- Section 9 lists the five out-of-scope items one per line.\\n- Format compliance: zero external citations / references (no paper authors or traceable prior-art names), all numeric placeholders marked with `.`, code blocks appear only in Section 2 (simulator state + env.step) and Section 3 (three policy parameter tables), markdown tables only in the threat list (Section 5) and the secondary-endpoints annex (Section 6), English-only.\\n\", \"assumptions\": [\"Fixed-policy evaluation per the locked-in resolution (NO RL training); the env returns r=0 unconditionally and policies do not consume reward.\", \"\\\"Hierarchical\\\" = options-framework temporal hierarchy \\u2014 meta-controller + named options + intra-option policies + per-option termination predicates \\u2014 with the same atomic action set as the flat policy; not module decomposition, not multi-agent command structure.\", \"Episode termination includes both pirate-induced death AND resource depletion. Other hazards (e.g., random environmental events, terrain damage) are absent from v1.\", \"SCORE_THRESHOLD is a single deterministic value per the calibration rule T = round(0.7 \\u00d7 pilot_mean_survival_H_OFF); the 5-episode H-OFF pilot runs before v1 and writes pilot_calibration.json. Threshold sweep is out of scope for v1 and is left for follow-on work.\", \"n=30 per cell floor; n extends to 60 only when the post-collection 95% CI half-width on the per-cell mean exceeds 15% of the pooled cross-cell mean of survival_ticks.\", \"Annex reporting contains two markdown tables only: Table A cause-of-death breakdown, Table B mean score at termination + mean pirate encounters survived. No narrative prose in the annex.\", \"No fifth \\\"true baseline\\\" condition (no pirates, no threshold logic). Matched-everything-else OFF is the control, and the verbatim justifying sentence is included in Section 5.\", \"The decision rule for ANOVA \\u2192 SSR-H substitution is the formal pair (Shapiro\\u2013Wilk on residuals at \\u03b1=0.05 OR Levene on cell variances at \\u03b1=0.05); the rule is named up front, not left as conditional on diagnostics.\", \"P-values on the F_AB interaction term come from a permutation test (5,000 shuffles, +1 numerator for non-degeneracy) because stdlib has no chi-squared distribution table.\", \"Three fixed policies \\u00d7 two deterministic architectures is the complete enumeration; no policy is sampled stochastically from a population.\", \"The simulator implementation is stdlib Python 3.12 only \\u2014 no torch, no numpy, no gym, no scipy, no network calls.\", \"MAX_TICKS = 5000 and is right-censoring at 10\\u00d7 expected median survival under H-OFF.\", \"The hierarchical architecture's options menu and intra-option policies are wired deterministically to the policy number (P1\\u2192aggressive-harvester / P2\\u2192stay_safe / P3\\u2192scout_cluster etc.); option structure is part of the policy implementation, not a free parameter.\", \"The pilot's 5 H-OFF seeds are reserved from the master seed in a disjoint range from v1 sub-seeds; no seed is reused across (cell, policy) tuples.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"[RESOLVED] Whether RL must be in scope \\u2014 fixed-policy evaluation accepted per the resolution; learning is follow-on work.\", \"[RESOLVED] Which operationalization of \\\"hierarchical\\\" \\u2014 options-framework temporal hierarchy accepted per the resolution; module decomposition and multi-agent command structure ruled out as confounders.\", \"[RESOLVED] Episode termination set \\u2014 pirate-induced death 